#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bin_lists

#% text
# `bin_lists(xs, w)` gives the lists of the real numbers of `xs` in the bins `[k w, (k + 1) w)` from the lowest to the highest occupied; `bin_lists(xs, [a, b, dx])` uses the bins `[a, a + dx)`, ... up to `b`; `bin_lists(xs, [[e1, e2, ...]])` the bins `[e1, e2)`, `[e2, e3)`, ....

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Elements that have no real numerical value are left out.

#% item
# The bins are those of `bin_counts`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
bin_lists([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], 2)
#% output
#= [[1, 1], [3, 2, 2], [4, 5], [6]]

#% cell
bin_lists([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], [0, 10, 1])
#% output
#= [[], [1, 1], [2, 2], [3], [4], [5], [6], [], [], []]

#% cell
bin_lists([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], [[-inf, 2, 5, 7, inf]])
#% output
#= [[1, 1], [3, 2, 4, 2], [5, 6], []]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `bin_counts`, `gather_by`
